deep.reconstruction:
Setting: a functor f: X → Y of small categories, with W the basic localizer (weak equivalences of nerves). The page catalogues classes of functors defined by homotopical or cohomological behaviour, in parallel columns related by the duality X ↦ X^op. This is the 'cohomological properties of maps in (Cat)' programme, a precursor of the proper/smooth functor theory in Pursuing Stacks and Les Dérivateurs.

(1, top) Class E (probably meant to be C, precofibred functors). Three equivalent conditions:
- X_b → X_{/b} is 'cofinal' (it has an adjoint);
- the comma functors a\X → b\Y have fibres with an initial object;
- for every arrow b→b' and every a in X_b, the category of lifts of b→b' with source a has an initial object. This is the existence of cocartesian lifts.
The dual column F (fibred functors) is left blank.

(2) Class Pr (proper). The conditions:
- X_b → X_{/b} is a 'strong' coaspherical equivalence, i.e. lies in As^0;
- equivalently, a\X → b\Y has aspherical fibres, i.e. lies in S;
- equivalently, the category of lifts of b→b' with given source a in X_b is aspherical (an aspherical relaxation of the cocartesian-lift condition);
- equivalently, Rf_* commutes with arbitrary base change. This is a proper base change theorem for presheaf topoi on Cat.
A bracket adds: equivalently, base change by f preserves strong equivalences. A circled margin note says a\X → b\Y is 'proper with aspherical(?) fibres'.
Dual column Sm (smooth): base change by f commutes with Rg_* (smooth base change).

(1) Class S: f such that base change along f preserves weak equivalences over Y. Modern name: W-sharp / universally homotopy-cartesian functors.

Relations recorded:
- F ⊊ Sm and C ⊊ Pr (fibrations are smooth, opfibrations are proper);
- Sm ∩ Pr ⊊ S, an 'Ehresmann theorem' in Cat;
- T∩Pr ⊂ S and T∩Sm ⊂ S, with T presumably 'locally trivial / locally constant fibres';
- the dualities C = F^0, F = C^0, S = S^0, Sm = Pr^0, Pr = Sm^0.

(3) WSE ('weakly sharp'). Definition (a): for all b→b' in Y, X_{/b} → X_{/b'} lies in W. This is exactly the hypothesis of Quillen's Theorem B. It is equivalent to:
- (b) pullback along f sends W-maps u: Y''→Y' between objects smooth over Y into W;
- (a') the cofibred replacement Cofib(X/Y) → Y (the Grothendieck construction of b ↦ X/b) lies in S.
WSE is stable under base change by smooth arrows, perhaps not by arbitrary g ∈ S. A margin note links it to Rf_*(constant coefficients) being locally constant, a cohomological Theorem B. The dual WSF uses b\X → b'\X and Fib(X/Y), and is stable under proper base change. Further claims: WSC∩Pr ⊂ S and WSF∩Sm ⊂ S.

(3bis) WSB, a two-sided version. For every chain b'_0 → b_0 →u b_1 → b'_1, the map of two-sided commas b'_0\X/b_1 → b'_0\X/b'_1 lies in W. Equivalently, f transforms the weak equivalences between two-sided commas of Y (X[u], X[u'], twisted-arrow-type data) into weak equivalences. This is claimed equivalent to both one-sided Theorem-B conditions holding locally on Y.

Purpose: to set up a six-operations-style dictionary (proper/smooth base change, local constancy, Ehresmann) for Cat as a model of homotopy types.
